'''Hunter Wonders''' (born August 7, 1998) is an American cross-country skier who has competed internationally in FIS competitions and the FIS Cross-Country World Cup. He has represented the United States in major international events including the World Championships and the Youth Olympic Winter Games. Wonders is a member of the U.S. cross-country skiing program and previously competed for the Alaska Pacific University Nordic Ski Center.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hunter Wonders – Athlete Biography |url=https://www.fis-ski.com/DB/general/athlete-biography.html?sectorcode=CC&competitorid=205730&type=result |access-date=March 8, 2026 |publisher=[[International Ski and Snowboard Federation]]}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Wonders was born in [[Anchorage, Alaska]], United States. He grew up in Alaska and began skiing at a young age, developing within the region's strong Nordic skiing culture. He later joined the [[Alaska Pacific University]] Nordic Ski Center program, a well-known training base for elite American cross-country skiers.<ref name=":0">{{cite web |date=May 8, 2023 |title=Wonders retires from World Cup cross-country ski racing |url=https://www.usskiandsnowboard.org/news/wonders-retires-world-cup-cross-country-ski-racing |access-date=March 8, 2026 |publisher=[[U.S. Ski & Snowboard]]}}</ref>

== Career ==
Wonders emerged as a competitive skier during his junior years. He competed at the [[2016 Winter Youth Olympics]] in Lillehammer, where he recorded several top-30 finishes. He later competed in the FIS Nordic Junior World Ski Championships, where he was part of the [[United States]] relay team that won a silver medal in the 4×5&nbsp;km event in 2018.<ref>{{cite web |date=February 9, 2026 |title=AOTW: Anchorage skier bounces back with Olympic bid |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2026/02/10/aotw-after-brief-retirement-2023-anchorage-skier-bounces-back-with-olympic-bid/ |access-date=March 8, 2026 |publisher=[[KTUU-TV|Alaska's News Source]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Saari |first=Matias |date=December 12, 2022 |title=Anchorage's Hunter Wonders records career-best World Cup finish in Norway |url=https://alaskasportsreport.com/2022/12/anchorages-hunter-wonders-records-career-best-world-cup-finish-in-norway/ |access-date=March 8, 2026 |work=Alaska Sports Report}}</ref>

=== Senior career ===
Wonders made his FIS World Cup debut on January 23, 2021 in the 30&nbsp;km skiathlon event in Lahti, [[Finland]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Hunter Wonders – Career information |url=https://www.fis-ski.com/DB/general/athlete-biography.html?competitorid=205730&sectorcode=CC&type=career |access-date=March 8, 2026 |publisher=International Ski and Snowboard Federation}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= |first= |date=February 18, 2021 |title=Anchorage skier Hunter Wonders added to US nordic team for World Championships |url=https://www.adn.com/sports/skiing/2021/02/18/anchorage-skier-hunter-wonders-added-to-us-nordic-team-for-world-championships/ |access-date=March 8, 2026 |work=Anchorage Daily News}}</ref>

He represented the United States at multiple international competitions including the FIS Nordic World Ski Championships and World Cup events. During the 2022–23 season he recorded several strong results on the World Cup circuit, including a career-best performance of 11th place in a distance event during the Tour de Ski.<ref>{{cite web |date=May 8, 2023 |title=Wonders retires from World Cup cross-country ski racing |url=https://www.usskiandsnowboard.org/news/wonders-retires-world-cup-cross-country-ski-racing |access-date=March 8, 2026 |publisher=U.S. Ski & Snowboard}}</ref>

Over his career he competed in a range of international competitions including World Cups, Nor-Am Cups, U23 World Championships, and U.S. National Championships.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hunter Wonders |url=https://www.fis-ski.com/DB/general/athlete-biography.html?sectorcode=CC&competitorid=205730&type=result |access-date=March 8, 2026 |publisher=International Ski and Snowboard Federation}}</ref>

=== Retirement and later activities ===
In 2023, Wonders announced that he would step away from full-time World Cup racing after several seasons with the U.S. team. During his career he became known for his steady improvement in distance events and regular top-30 finishes at the World Cup level.<ref name=":0" />

Outside of skiing, Wonders has pursued aviation training and has worked toward obtaining a private pilot license.<ref name=":0" />

== Personal life ==
Wonders grew up in Anchorage, Alaska. In addition to skiing, he has interests in outdoor activities such as cycling, hiking, and backcountry skiing.<ref name=":0" />

